 THE FIRST IS THAT OUR TLS CLIENT TALKS TO A FOREIGN SERVER, which had
 never been shown. `build/tls-interop-test.ps1` drives our SERVER with
 openssl's client, so the client half was exercised only by our own
 server, in a loopback -- the same one-directional shape the compression
 post-mortem is about, pointed at TLS. Here OpenSSL is the server and the
 handshake, the certificate chain and the record layer are all its idea of
 the RFC rather than ours.

 THE SECOND IS THAT AN IoT SESSION CAN BE SECURED. `MqttEndpoint` and
 `TlsEndpoint` are both pure byte endpoints, so composing them is a
 caller's job and not a protocol change: MQTT packets become TLS
 application data, and TLS records become TCP payload. This is that
 caller, and until it existed the CRA claim in `docs/KingsAndCourts.md`
 about protected data in transit had no program behind it.

 THERE ARE THREE FRAMINGS STACKED HERE and each one has to be unpicked
 separately, which is the whole difficulty. TCP delivers bytes; TLS
 records sit in those bytes with a five-byte header; MQTT packets sit in
 the decrypted stream with a variable-length header. A layer that assumes
 its input arrives whole is wrong at every level.

